[{"id":5022666590,"node_id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDUwMjI2NjY1OTA=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/events/5022666590","actor":{"login":"lukasjuhrich","id":10060406,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEwMDYwNDA2","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/10060406?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich","html_url":"https://github.com/lukasjuhrich","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukasjuhrich/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"event":"labeled","commit_id":null,"commit_url":null,"created_at":"2021-07-15T01:19:27Z","label":{"name":"requires triage","color":"ffff00"},"performed_via_github_app":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/880347878","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/6769#issuecomment-880347878","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/6769","id":880347878,"node_id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDg4MDM0Nzg3OA==","user":{"login":"zzzeek","id":128223,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyODIyMw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/128223?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek","html_url":"https://github.com/zzzeek","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2021-07-15T02:49:56Z","updated_at":"2021-07-15T02:49:56Z","body":"> Not sure if Bug or question, but at least unexpected behavior.\r\n> \r\n> **Describe the bug**\r\n> When querying duplicated results of the type `(User, int, datetime)`, an attempted deduplication using `.unique()` did not have any effect, and returned multiple rows containing the exact same values.\r\n> \r\n> The reason is that the default identification strategy amounts to an element-wise projection using `id`, and not `hash`.\r\n\r\nthis is not accurate.  the default deduplication strategy in the ORM is this:\r\n\r\n```\r\n        _unique_filters=[\r\n            _no_unique\r\n            if context.yield_per\r\n            else id\r\n            if ent.use_id_for_hash\r\n            else None\r\n            for ent in context.compile_state._entities\r\n        ],\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nignoring the \"yield_per\" part, what this means is that for each \"entity\" in the ORM select, in your example User, int, and date, has a deduplication strategy of its own based on what kind of entity it is. For the ORM entity \"User\", it uses id(), because there's an [identity map](https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/14/glossary.html?highlight=identity%20map#term-identity-map) so id() is in fact uniquing.  for the other values, it uses hash().   The program below illustrates this:\r\n\r\n\r\n```py\r\nimport datetime\r\n\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import DateTime\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import Integer\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import select\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import union_all\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session, aliased\r\n\r\nBase = declarative_base()\r\n\r\n\r\nclass A(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"a\"\r\n\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n    data = Column(Integer)\r\n    date = Column(DateTime)\r\n\r\n\r\ne = create_engine(\"sqlite://\", echo=False)\r\nBase.metadata.create_all(e)\r\n\r\ns = Session(e)\r\n\r\ns.add(A(id=1, data=5, date=datetime.datetime.now()))\r\ns.commit()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef go():\r\n\r\n    union = aliased(\r\n        A,\r\n        union_all(\r\n            select(A),\r\n            select(A),\r\n            select(A),\r\n        ).subquery(),\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    result = s.execute(select(union, union.data, union.date))\r\n    return result\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(\"\\n\\nno deduplication:\")\r\nprint(go().all())\r\n\r\nprint(\"\\n\\ndeduplication:\")\r\nprint(go().unique().all())\r\n```\r\n\r\nthe output from the program looks like:\r\n\r\n```py\r\n$ python test3.py \r\n\r\nno deduplication:\r\n[(<__main__.A object at 0x7f598f6293a0>, 5, datetime.datetime(2021, 7, 14, 22, 36, 11, 886564)), (<__main__.A object at 0x7f598f6293a0>, 5, datetime.datetime(2021, 7, 14, 22, 36, 11, 886564)), (<__main__.A object at 0x7f598f6293a0>, 5, datetime.datetime(2021, 7, 14, 22, 36, 11, 886564))]\r\n\r\n\r\ndeduplication:\r\n[(<__main__.A object at 0x7f598f638100>, 5, datetime.datetime(2021, 7, 14, 22, 36, 11, 886564))]\r\n```\r\n\r\nso we can see that unique() is in fact deduplicating the row using value for the int / date values.\r\n\r\n> \r\n> I can provide context from a debugging session. I'm breaking in `ResultInternal._allrows` in [this line](https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/blob/rel_1_4_18/lib/sqlalchemy/engine/result.py#L409).\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> >>> type(self)\r\n> <class 'sqlalchemy.engine.result.ChunkedIteratorResult'>\r\n> >>> self._metadata._unique_filters\r\n> [<built-in function id>, <built-in function id>, <built-in function id>]\r\n\r\nso that looks like the result you'd get from a row consisting of three ORM objects.    i dont know how you'd get this from what you describe.   try out my example to see.\r\n\r\n```py\r\nprint(\"\\nthe filters:\")\r\nprint(go()._metadata._unique_filters)\r\n```\r\n\r\nreturns:\r\n\r\n```\r\nthe filters:\r\n[<built-in function id>, None, None]\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n> >>> [tuple(id(el) for el in r) for r in made_rows[:4]]\r\n> [(140281848273408, 140281848246224, 140281848184384),\r\n> (140281848273408, 140281848267056, 140281848184480),\r\n> (140281848273552, 140281848267344, 140281848184672),\r\n> (140281848273552, 140281848267632, 140281848270896)]\r\n> >>> [tuple(hash(el) for el in r) for r in made_rows[:4]]\r\n> [(8767615517088, 13812, -7323339362369131990),\r\n> (8767615517088, 13812, -7323339362369131990),\r\n> (8767615517097, 13815, -4473479583634129130),\r\n> (8767615517097, 13815, -4473479583634129130)]\r\n> >>> [tuple(type(el) for el in r) for r in made_rows[:4]]\r\n> [(<class 'pycroft.model.user.User'>, <class 'int'>, <class 'datetime.datetime'>),\r\n> (<class 'pycroft.model.user.User'>, <class 'int'>, <class 'datetime.datetime'>),\r\n\r\nso yeah, there's no way I can think of that this would be possible, so please take the example program above and modify it such that it produces what you are seeing.\r\n","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/880347878/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"zzzeek","id":128223,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyODIyMw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/128223?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek","html_url":"https://github.com/zzzeek","foll